PUBLIC RESPONSE: D. Atrial septal defect

The auscultatory complex is a right-sided systolic ejection murmur. A harsh systolic ejection murmur maximal at the left upper sternal border / 2nd-3rd interspace, radiating toward the left clavicle/supraclavicular area, increasing with inspiration and associated with a palpable thrill in the pulmonary area is the pulmonary flow/ejection murmur.

That is exactly what is produced by a left-to-right atrial shunt with increased pulmonary flow. In an ASD pulmonary blood flow is increased, the pulmonary valve is traversed by a higher volume and velocity, and the resulting systolic ejection murmur is heard best at the LUSB, radiates to the left clavicle, is inspiratory augmented because right ventricular filling increases with inspiration, and can be associated with a thrill when the shunt is large. Chronic left-to-right shunting gives exertional dyspnoea, fatigue, light-headedness in a young adult, with a remote history of faint cyanosis in childhood compatible with a long-standing congenital shunt with eventual pulmonary vascular changes.

The ECG supports a chronic right-sided load with right ventricular hypertrophy, and the reported left axis deviation is not typical of secundum ASD with right axis deviation/incomplete RBBB; it is the pattern classically quoted for an ostium primum / AV canal type ASD, which is still an atrial level left-to-right shunt and gives the same increased pulmonary flow systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border with inspiratory augmentation and RVH.

Pulmonic valvular stenosis gives the same bedside description, but valvular pulmonary stenosis is not an offered choice. Among the listed lesions the only one that can reproduce a left upper sternal border inspiratory-augmented systolic ejection pulmonary flow murmur with RVH and a young adult presentation of exertional intolerance is an atrial septal defect.

The other options are inconsistent with the finding:
Ebstein anomaly is tricuspid regurgitation/holosystolic murmur with right atrial enlargement;
PDA is continuous machinery murmur at the left upper sternal border;
Mitral valve prolapse is a late systolic click/murmur at the apex;
HCM with LVOT obstruction is a dynamic LVOT systolic murmur that varies with Valsalva and is best at left lower sternal border/apex;
Tricuspid stenosis is a diastolic rumble;
VSD is a harsh holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border/apex.

Hence, on the choice list provided, the intended lesion that reproduces the described pulmonary ejection murmur with inspiratory augmentation, thrill, RVH and left axis deviation is an atrial septal defect, primum type.

